<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Venantius Fortunatus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 530–c. 609</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> · Also known as Venantius Honorius Clementianus Fortunatus, Venance Fortunat, Saint Venantius Fortunatus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Venantius Fortunatus was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> poet and hymn writer at the Merovingian courts and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poitiers">Poitiers</a>, venerated as a saint. Born between 530 and 540 near Treviso in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and educated in Ravenna, he came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a> in 566 and performed a poem at the wedding of King Sigibert and Queen Brunhild. At Poitiers he became a close friend of Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radegund">Radegund</a>, writing poems in her honour, and enjoyed the patronage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gregory-of-tours">Gregory of Tours</a>. His eleven surviving books of poetry include panegyrics and epitaphs, and his hymns Pange lingua gloriosi proelium certaminis and Vexilla Regis prodeunt entered the Catholic liturgy. He became Bishop of Poitiers around 599–600 and died early in the 7th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/venantius-fortunatus.jpg" alt="Venantius Fortunatus" width="330" height="334" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Venantius_Fortunatus_miniature.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its author is unknown: it has been attributed to <b>Venantius Fortunatus</b> in the 6th century, Hermannus Contractus in the 11th and Bernard of Clairvaux in the 12th.</blockquote><a href="/songs/ave-maris-stella">Ave maris stella</a></li><li><blockquote>Its opening words echo an older and famous Latin hymn, &quot;Pange lingua gloriosi proelium certaminis&quot; by <b>Venantius Fortunatus</b>, from which it is derived.</blockquote><a href="/songs/pange-lingua-gloriosi-corporis-mysterium">Pange lingua gloriosi corporis mysterium</a></li></ul></section>
